# @installer — the graphical installer, and nothing else (plan/16).
#
# ONE ATOM. Everything else in the ~25-package tail (boost, grub, os-prober, squashfs-tools,
# fuse, yaml-cpp, cracklib, libpwquality, dmidecode, efivar, efibootmgr, mandoc, sudo, rsync)
# arrives as a dependency and is resolved, not listed: naming transitive deps in a set is how a
# set stops describing intent and starts duplicating the resolver.
#
# THE POINT OF THIS FILE IS THAT NOTHING ELSE INCLUDES IT. This set is named only by
# config/profiles/installer.conf, whose PROFILE_ROLE is "live": the image it produces is booted
# from removable media and thrown away. The desktop profile never emerges @installer, and
# config/portage/expected-packages.desktop.txt does not list one package of the tail, so stage 50
# fails the build if any of it ever reaches the installable product. That assertion — not this
# comment — is what keeps the promise (plan/16 §3.3).
#
# What the tail costs, and why it is only acceptable here: a UKI-booting, systemd-boot, EROFS
# distro has no use whatsoever for GRUB (built with the legacy-BIOS platform, plus Gentoo's GRUB
# theme artwork), os-prober or squashfs-tools. They are unconditional RDEPENDs of
# app-admin/calamares with no USE flag to turn them off. On the install medium that is a shrug;
# in the product image it would be indefensible.
#
# GRUB was the expensive part of that shrug — 68.4 MiB installed, 41.6 MiB of EROFS — so it is
# no longer shrugged at: the PACKAGE still arrives, because nothing can stop it, but stage 50
# section 3j deletes its files on any live profile (plan/20 §2.3). Both halves of the claim are
# checked rather than assumed: this medium boots a UKI, and the `exec:` sequence in
# config/calamares/settings.conf.in names our `imagebootloader` module and never the stock
# `bootloader`/`grubcfg`. os-prober and squashfs-tools are left alone — they measure 0.0 and
# 0.5 MiB, and deleting a package's contents for no bytes only adds a path that can stop
# matching without anyone noticing.
#
# ~amd64, and mandatory rather than a preference: both STABLE calamares revisions cap at
# PYTHON_COMPAT=python3_{11..13} and this image is on python 3.14 (plan/06), so the stable
# ebuild does not resolve at all — REQUIRED_USE "exactly-one-of ( python_single_target_python3_12
# python_single_target_python3_13 )" is unsatisfiable. The alternative, adding python3_13 as a
# second PYTHON_TARGET, would put a second interpreter in the image and is strictly worse. See
# config/portage/package.accept_keywords/image.
#
# USE=-livecd deliberately, at the ebuild default: that flag applies a large downstream patch set
# aimed at Gentoo's own stage3/portage live medium (the same audience as
# app-admin/calamares-gentoo-livecd, which is NOT what this is). Every step it exists to help
# with — unpack a stage3, configure portage, install GRUB — is something this distro does not
# have. Installing here is `dd` (plan/16 §5.1), and the modules that do it are ours.
app-admin/calamares

# ---- the accounts page (plan/21) ------------------------------------------------------------
# The installer's only account-creation page: local accounts, a managed system, or an enterprise
# domain, as one choice. Calamares accepts ONLY C++ QtPlugin view modules (ModuleFactory.cpp:53),
# so a page cannot be a script — which is why this is an ebuild in config/portage/overlay and why
# the JOB beside it is an ordinary python module in config/calamares/local-modules. Its UI is QML
# with Kirigami, hosted in a QQuickWidget inside that plugin; the compiled part is thin.
#
# MANDATORY, unlike the enrolment page it replaces. That one was optional and settings.conf
# carried a substituted token so a medium without it still built; this one is what creates the
# account, so stage 40 refuses to build an installer medium whose lock did not carry it.
#
# Named only by this profile: on a desktop image it would install a Calamares plugin into a
# system with no Calamares. Harmless, and still wrong — the package audit would then have to
# explain why an installer plugin is on a machine that cannot run the installer.
distro-base/distro-calamares-accounts

# ---- the language page (plan/22) ------------------------------------------------------------
# The installer's FIRST page, and the module that owns the six requirement checks. It replaces the
# stock `welcome` module, whose order is in its C++ — WelcomePage.cpp inserts the requirements
# checker above the language row, and ResultsListWidget swaps that list for an expanding logo when
# everything passes, so on a healthy machine the language picker is a closed combo box at the
# bottom of a page of English. No configuration key reaches any of that.
#
# It is NOT called `welcome`: calamares_add_plugin installs a viewmodule into
# <libdir>/calamares/modules/<name>/, so a plugin of that name would collide file-for-file with
# app-admin/calamares' own and be blocked by portage.
#
# MANDATORY for two reasons rather than one: it is the first entry in settings.conf's sequence, and
# it is where QQuickStyle::setStyle() happens. That second one is why its absence would not merely
# remove a page: setStyle is ignored once anything has imported Qt Quick Controls, so the accounts
# page would come up in Fusion with no icons.
#
# Named only by this profile, like the accounts page: on a desktop image it would install a
# Calamares plugin into a system with no Calamares.
distro-base/distro-calamares-language

# ---- the greeting page (plan/23) ------------------------------------------------------------
# The installer's SECOND page — the greeting, and the verdict on whether this computer can install
# at all — and the module that owns the six requirement checks. It was the language page's second
# screen until plan/23; a view step is one entry in the sidebar, so the installer's first two
# questions appeared as a single step and the greeting could not be reached from the sidebar at all.
#
# Its requirements box is three source files VENDORED from Calamares' own welcome module
# (files/checker/). They are not in libcalamaresui and no header of theirs is installed, so a module
# that wants the box has to carry the source; the alternative was a fourth drawing of a widget every
# Calamares installer already shows.
#
# It is NOT called `welcome`, for the same file-collision reason as its sibling — and because
# ModuleManager takes the first module.desc it finds for a name across modules-search, so two
# modules called `welcome` would resolve by list order rather than by intent.
#
# MANDATORY: it contributes the checks that decide whether Next may be pressed at all, including
# the disk check that upstream silently drops on a Calamares built without libparted (plan/22 §3a).
# A medium without it would offer to install onto a 16 GiB disk and fail at the partition step.
distro-base/distro-calamares-greeting

# ---- the disk page (plan/24) ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The page that chooses a disk, and the module that replaced stock `partition` outright rather than
# configuring it into something else. That module was kept and reconfigured for the whole of Phase
# A — `allowManualPartitioning: false` plus a fixed `partitionLayout` leaves a device combo box and
# an Erase radio button — and what it left on screen was a partition editor with most of its
# controls taken away, using upstream's words for an installer that offers manual partitioning,
# side-by-side installs and filesystem choices. This distro has none of those.
#
# It is NOT called `partition`, for the same file-collision reason as its three siblings, and
# because ModuleManager takes the first module.desc it finds for a name across modules-search: two
# modules of one name would resolve by list order, with nothing in the log saying which won.
#
# MANDATORY. It is the only page that chooses a disk, and the `disksetup` job reads its answer out
# of GlobalStorage — so a medium without it runs an installer that reaches the accounts page and
# then fails in the exec phase with no device to write to. Stage 40 refuses to build one.
#
# What it does NOT drag in is the point of the atom: no kpmcore, no libparted, no udisks. The page
# reads /sys/block and asks lsblk for each row's second line; the job runs sfdisk and mkfs. The
# dependency it replaces was upstream's KPMcore backend, which arrives with app-admin/calamares
# either way and is now simply never used.
#
# Named only by this profile, like the other three: on a desktop image it would install a Calamares
# plugin into a system with no Calamares.
distro-base/distro-calamares-disk

# ---- the applications page (plan/25) -----------------------------------------------------------
# The page that asks which extra applications to add from Flathub, and the last question the
# installer asks. It replaces nothing — no stock module asks this — which is also why it is the
# one page whose absence the install survives in full: the payload's own applications are already
# on the disk, offline the page forces "nothing extra" anyway, and the `appsetup` job that does
# the downloading may not fail an install for a download that did not finish.
#
# MANDATORY all the same, for the reason that makes every sibling mandatory: settings.conf names
# it, Calamares silently drops any step whose module is installed nowhere, and the shape of THAT
# failure is an installer that never mentions applications and never updates the ones it ships —
# a promise the medium makes on its applications page that the medium then breaks.
#
# Named only by this profile, like the other four: on a desktop image it would install a
# Calamares plugin into a system with no Calamares.
distro-base/distro-calamares-apps
